      "question": {
        ":question/id": "f44288d9-8336-5a83-aa76-0f3bde6d32ff",
        ":question/problem": "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2,5)$ and $(7,3)$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,5)$ and $(7,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5}{7-2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (- \\frac{2}{5})=-1\\,\u21d2\\,m=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(6,-5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =\\frac{5}{2}(x-6) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{5 x}{2} - 15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5 x}{2} - 20\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
